{"id":"u-6a05de11-f214-4c51-83ff-f993dc90bffd","src":"community","title":"Progressive Recomp","author":"Jim","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/BPZAyM-ai-reco","desc":"Build Strength. Lose Fat. Become Better.\n\nProgressive Recomp is a 5-day resistance-training program designed to help beginners build muscle, increase strength, and gradually reduce body fat through structured training, progressive overload, proper recovery, and sustainable nutrition.\n\nThe program is built around the principle that meaningful physical transformation comes from consistent progress rather than extreme workouts or restrictive dieting. The goal is to improve body composition by developing lean muscle while gradually reducing excess body fat.\n\n1. Build a Strong Foundation\n\nAs a beginner, the priority is developing proper exercise technique, consistency, and training habits. The program focuses on fundamental movements and controlled repetitions rather than unnecessarily heavy weights or advanced techniques.\n\nThe objective is to become better at each exercise over time while learning how to train safely and effectively.\n\n2. Progressive Overload\n\nProgressive overload is the foundation of the program. Instead of simply completing workouts, you are encouraged to gradually improve your performance by increasing repetitions, weight, control, or technique.\n\nFor example, once you can comfortably complete the upper end of an exercise's prescribed rep range with good form, you can gradually increase the weight and work your way up again.\n\nThe goal is simple: give your body a reason to adapt and become stronger.\n\n3. Build Muscle While Losing Fat\n\nProgressive Recomp aims for body recomposition rather than an aggressive bulk or cut.\n\nThe goal is:\n\nMore muscle + less body fat = better body composition.\n\nBecause beginners can often make significant strength and muscle gains when starting resistance training, the program combines consistent lifting with appropriate nutrition and activity levels to support gradual fat loss while preserving or building muscle.\n\n4. Balanced Muscle Development\n\nThe weekly structure provides repeated training opportunities for the major upper-body muscle groups while keeping Saturday dedicated to legs.\n\nMonday: Push — Chest, shoulders, triceps\nTuesday: Pull — Back, rear delts, biceps\nWednesday: Rest\nThursday: Upper — Chest, back, shoulders, arms\nFriday: Arms + Back + Chest\nSaturday: Legs — Quads, hamstrings, glutes, calves, core\nSunday: Rest\n\nTraining volume is distributed throughout the week to provide sufficient stimulus without unnecessarily overwhelming recovery.\n\n5. Prioritize Recovery\n\nTraining is only the stimulus for growth. Recovery is where adaptation occurs.\n\nWednesday and Sunday are dedicated recovery days, while the back sessions are carefully distributed to avoid excessive fatigue. Rest periods, appropriate training intensity, sleep, and nutrition all contribute to long-term progress.\n\n6. Long-Term Progress\n\nProgressive Recomp is designed to be more than a collection of exercises. It teaches you how to train with purpose by understanding sets, repetitions, rest, intensity, progressive overload, and recovery.","level":["Novice"],"goals":["Muscle","Bodyweight Fitness","Strength"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":70,"joins":1,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-10-14T16:33:08.722Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["MONDAY — PUSH","TUESDAY — PULL","THURSDAY — UPPER BODY","FRIDAY — ARMS + BACK + CHEST","SATURDAY — LEG DAY"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"n":"Chest Fly (Machine)","sets":[{"r":15,"x":3}]},{"n":"Incline Bench Press (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"x":3}]},{"n":"Bench Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"x":3}]},{"n":"Seated Shoulder Press (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"x":3}]},{"n":"Lateral Raise (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":10,"x":3}]},{"n":"Tricep Pushdown (Cable)","sets":[{"r":10,"x":3}]},{"n":"Overhead Tricep Extension (Cable)","sets":[{"r":10,"x":3}]}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Lat P